  for republicans  when some tea party candidates were  elected in the 2010 midterms to the  house of representatives i suggested one  of two things is eventually going to  happen with this movement either  that is just a blip in terms of those  tea party views and they're going to be  pushed back out and they'll be gone from  the republican party  or they'll become integrated into the  republican party and no longer be much  of a separate group  and that's essentially what happened you  do have a tea party caucus in the house  of representatives  it's not very prominent they have 23  seats in the house  modest but not insignificant and some  sort of more libertarian don't tread on  me ideas have infiltrated  the republican party big picture but  it's not dramatically  changed the republican party whenever  trump ultimately leaves  the american right is going to have to  make a decision about the direction they  want to go  the decision is not likely to be made by  some kind of central meeting  it's more likely a decision that's going  to be made implicitly by  who they vote for to be their nominee in  2024  who they vote for in the house who they  vote for in the senate  so some of the questions that come up  are  as an example is there a future in the  republican party  that bridges the sort of mitt romney  pro-business right  the religious right and the liberty  freedom right which  are the three groups that i've  identified previously  with what i am calling the fourth wing  of the right  which is the i i don't want to be  pejorative but it's like the reactionary  anti-intellectuals it's trumpism  essentially and  i don't really know what it should be  called this fourth group  it's a group that doesn't really care  about policy you've noticed that under  trump  it's not a particularly religious group  at least not by definition  but they basically see trump as a genius  and they favor doing things that just  trigger the libs it's not about getting  things done 